    "claims": [
      {
        "text": "The industry is not headed for a blanket cut in fees; McKinsey expects fee pressure to be uneven, showing up as client demands for transparency and unbundled pricing rather than lower basis points.",
        "chunk_ids": [
          "MCK-SIGNAL-SELLOFF-2026#9"
        ],
        "part_id": "a1.all"
      },
      {
        "text": "Cerulli reports that for the past decade financial firms have shifted compensation models from largely commission-based to an asset-related fee structure, while Edward Jones attributes falling mutual fund and insurance revenue to ongoing fee compression.",
        "chunk_ids": [
          "CERULLI-AFFLUENT-FEE#0",
          "EDJ-10K-2025#151"
        ],
        "part_id": "a1.all"
      },
      {
        "text": "Fee transparency is the dominant client expectation, with a Wealthtender survey finding 73% of participants called it crucial to an advisor's credibility and 61.8% cited fee structure and pricing as the most helpful information.",
        "chunk_ids": [
          "WEALTHTENDER-ADVISOR-SEARCH-2025#15",
          "WEALTHTENDER-ADVISOR-SEARCH-2025#36"
        ],
        "part_id": "a2.all"
      },
      {
        "text": "Regulatory pressure is shaping transparency through disclosures like Form CRS and Regulation Best Interest, and through SEC cash sweep enforcement, including a $187 million settlement with Charles Schwab in 2022.",
        "chunk_ids": [
          "RJF-10K-2025#49",
          "SCHW-TRADE-2026-09-30-IN-01#3"
        ],
        "part_id": "a3.all"
      },
      {
        "text": "Competitive pressure is driving firms to cut prices, with Morgan Stanley, Schwab, and Edward Jones each stating they face pricing pressure from competitors reducing fees, eliminating commissions, or offering robo-advice.",
        "chunk_ids": [
          "MS-10K-2025#110",
          "SCHW-10K-2025#95",
          "EDJ-10K-2025#26"
        ],
        "part_id": "a4.all"
      },
      {
        "text": "On data fees the comparison can be made on this evidence: trade press reports Schwab introduced data fees over seven years ago and UBS and Wells Fargo in the mid-2010s and 2020 respectively, while Merrill is later than many peers, though Merrill has newly created data and shelf fees.",
        "chunk_ids": [
          "MER-TRADE-2026-09-29-RB-01#7",
          "MER-TRADE-2026-09-29-RB-01#8",
          "MER-TRADE-2026-09-29-RB-01#5",
          "MER-TRADE-2026-09-29-RB-01#1"
        ],
        "part_id": "a6"
      }
    ],
